"People have baked bread for thousands of years without knowing why it rose. In 1680, Anton van Leeuwenhoek saw yeast through a microscope for the first time. Almost two hundred years later, Louis Pasteur showed that living yeast does the work. So the next time you eat a steamed bun, say thank you to all those tiny helpers.": "24deeef9654d",
"Soda is full of tiny bubbles.": "2962702469f3",
"Tear open a steamed bun or a slice of toast and look closely. It is full of tiny holes, like a sponge. A dumpling wrapper is made from flour and water too, but it is flat and thin, with no holes at all. So who put the holes in the bread?": "08bcd8e033e6",
"Tear open a steamed bun or a slice of toast and look closely. It is full of tiny holes, like a sponge. A dumpling wrapper is made from flour and water too, but it is flat and thin, with no holes at all. So who put the holes in the bread? The answer is yeast. Yeast is not a chemical. It is a tiny living thing, a kind of fungus, and each one is a single cell far too small to see. The dry yeast in a packet looks like sand because the yeast is asleep. Give it warm water and a little food, and it wakes up. Yeast loves sugar, and there is sugar in dough. As the yeast eats, it gives off a gas called carbon dioxide, the same gas that makes the bubbles in soda. It also makes a little alcohol. Countless yeast cells are doing this all through the dough at the same time. Why doesn't the gas just float away? When you mix flour with water and knead it, a stretchy net called gluten forms inside the dough. The gluten traps the gas in little bubbles, like thousands of tiny balloons. As the balloons fill, the whole dough swells. In a warm place the yeast works fast. In the refrigerator it works very slowly. Then comes the heat. In a hot oven or a steamer, the gas in every bubble swells once more, and the dough gets even bigger. Soon the dough sets firm, the heat stops the yeast, and the alcohol evaporates. The bubbles cannot shrink back. They stay as the soft holes you see in bread and steamed buns. People have baked bread for thousands of years without knowing why it rose. In 1680, Anton van Leeuwenhoek saw yeast through a microscope for the first time. Almost two hundred years later, Louis Pasteur showed that living yeast does the work. So the next time you eat a steamed bun, say thank you to all those tiny helpers.": "403c189b310f",
